<DocsLayout title="Install. Settle in. Make it yours." section="start" summary="Install LunaDash with the session installer or the NixOS module, sign in and find your way around the first session.">
  <h2 id="linux">Install on Linux</h2>
  <p>LunaDash is developed primarily on Arch Linux. The session installer also knows the Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora, openSUSE, Alpine, Void and Gentoo package managers. It installs the dependencies (including Rust, which builds the SlintLu shell runtime), builds the compositor, the <a href="https://github.com/LunaDashWM/LunaDash-Shell">LunaDash shell</a> and <a href="../../slintlu/">SlintLu</a> at the revisions LunaDash pins, and installs the login session.</p>
  <CodeBlock code={'git clone --branch dev https://github.com/LuYishan-4/LunaDash.git\ncd LunaDash\n./scripts/install-session.sh'} />
  <p>On Arch the installer builds a package with makepkg and installs it with pacman; the other distributions build with Ninja and install through CMake. Preview the steps with <code>./scripts/install-session.sh --dry-run</code>; if your package manager is unknown, install the dependencies yourself and use <code>--skip-deps</code>. Video wallpapers additionally need <code>mpvpaper</code> (AUR on Arch).</p>
  <p>To update, pull the <code>dev</code> branch and run the installer again, or use <code>lunadash-update</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="nixos">Install on NixOS</h2>
  <p>The flake provides a NixOS module and packages. Import <code>lunadash.nixosModules.default</code> into your system configuration and enable the desktop:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={'programs.lunadash.enable = true;'} />
  <p>Rebuild, log out and select LunaDash in your display manager. Updates and rollback use NixOS generations. See the <a href="https://github.com/LuYishan-4/LunaDash/blob/dev/docs/en/NIXOS.md">NixOS guide</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="first">Your first session</h2>
  <p>Choose <strong>LunaDash</strong> in your display manager (greetd, SDDM, GDM …). The first login opens a short setup for language, network and appearance. After that you find:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the <strong>panel</strong> with workspaces, the window switcher button, the clock and system status;</li>
    <li>the <strong>dock</strong> with pinned applications and running windows (right-click pins and unpins);</li>
    <li>the <strong>launcher</strong> (tap Super, or Super+D) and the <strong>dashboard</strong> (the moon button);</li>
    <li><strong>Settings</strong> (the gear button) for everything from accent colours to tiling.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Super+T opens your terminal, Super+E the file manager and Super+W the wallpaper library. The <a href="../desktop/">desktop guide</a> explains workspaces, tiling and the switchers.</p>

  <h2 id="shells">Another shell, or none</h2>
  <p><code>~/.config/LunaDash/session.json</code> chooses what runs with the compositor: the LunaDash shell (default), any other command such as Waybar, or nothing, plus autostart commands. See <a href="../shell/#session">Customize the shell</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="limits">Understand the preview</h2>
  <p>LunaDash is a development preview. Physical GPUs, multiple outputs and some input-method and protocol details still vary between systems. Native compositor plugins stay disabled until you trust them and run without a sandbox.</p>
</DocsLayout>
